# {{ .Name }} ({{ .Type | title }})

Provides an API key (v2) resource. Requires API version `v3.12` or higher.

Unlike `wallix-bastion_apikey`, this v2 resource associates the key with a `profile`, which
determines the permissions the key is granted when used against the REST API.

**Important**: the key is stored in clear in the Terraform state. Protect the state backend as
you would protect the key (see the `apikey` attribute in the usage notes).

## Example Usage

```terraform
# API key with a built-in profile, restricted to specific source IPs
resource "wallix-bastion_apikey_v2" "monitoring" {
  apikey_name   = "monitoring"
  profile       = "auditor"
  ip_limitation = "10.10.11.22,10.10.33.44"
}

# API key with a custom profile, which must be read-only
resource "wallix-bastion_profile" "audit_api" {
  profile_name = "audit_api"
  editable     = false

  gui_features {
    wab_audit = "view"
  }

  gui_transmission {}
}

resource "wallix-bastion_apikey_v2" "audit_api" {
  apikey_name = "audit_api"
  profile     = wallix-bastion_profile.audit_api.profile_name
  description = "Used by the audit export job"

  # A change to the read-only profile replaces it, which the Bastion refuses while a key uses it.
  lifecycle {
    replace_triggered_by = [wallix-bastion_profile.audit_api]
  }
}

# Hand the key to its consumer through a secret store, in the same configuration:
# resource "<secret store resource>" "audit_api" {
#   value = wallix-bastion_apikey_v2.audit_api.apikey
# }
```

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Usage Notes

### The `apikey` attribute

The Bastion returns the key once, in the response to the creation request; afterwards, the API
only returns `********`. The provider stores the key in `apikey` at creation and keeps it on
refresh, so it can be passed to another resource, for example a secret in a secret store, in the
same configuration.

`apikey` is sensitive: plans hide it, and an `output` that references it needs
`sensitive = true`. It is still stored in clear in the Terraform state, so anyone who can read
the state can use the key. Use a state backend with encryption at rest and restricted access,
and prefer handing the key over through a secret store to reading it from the state.

The key can't be recovered from the Bastion: an imported key has an empty `apikey`, and a key
created by provider 0.17.0 or older keeps `********`. Replace the key
(`terraform apply -replace=wallix-bastion_apikey_v2.<name>`) to store a usable one; the old key
stops working.

### Profile

`profile` must reference an existing profile name and determines what the API key is authorized
to do when used against the REST API.

The Bastion only accepts a read-only profile, and refuses any other with `409 The API key must
be associated to a read-only profile`. Read-only means that the profile itself can't be modified
(`editable` is `false`), not that its permissions are limited to `view`: all the built-in
profiles are read-only, `product_administrator` included, while a profile created through the API
is editable unless it is created with `editable = false` (see `wallix-bastion_profile`). Choose
the least privileged profile that covers the use of the key.

The Bastion refuses any change to a read-only profile, so a change to a `wallix-bastion_profile`
with `editable = false` replaces it, and it refuses to delete a profile still associated with an
API key. `replace_triggered_by`, as in the example, replaces the key with the profile, which gives
a new key value.

### Immutable Fields

`profile` and `ip_limitation` can only be set at creation time: the Bastion API rejects attempts
to change them via update. Changing either in Terraform forces recreation of the key, which gives
a new key value.

### IP Limitation

`ip_limitation` is a comma-separated list of IP addresses allowed to use the key. Leave it unset
(or empty) to allow use from any IP.

## Import

API key can be imported using its `apikey_name`, e.g.

```shell
terraform import wallix-bastion_apikey_v2.monitoring monitoring
```

The imported `apikey` is empty: the Bastion never returns the key after its creation.
